{{> Top frame}}
<header>
  <p class="kicker">Lesson 1</p>
  <h1>Markup</h1>
  <p class="lede">A template is HTML with a few tags in double braces. roux compiles it to
  bytecode that the server runs; your Roc never sees the markup.</p>
</header>

<figure class="preview">
  <figcaption><code>templates/Hello.rocstache</code>, rendered</figcaption>
{{> Hello hello}}</figure>

<section class="turn-box">
  <h2>Your turn</h2>
  <p>Open <code>templates/Hello.rocstache</code>. It is one line:</p>
  <pre><code>&lt;p class="hello"&gt;Hello, &lt;strong&gt;&#123;&#123; name &#125;&#125;&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre>
  <p>Change the words around the tag: make it <em>Good evening</em>, add an emoji, wrap
  something in <code>&lt;em&gt;</code>. Save, and watch the box above.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>How this page is made</h2>
  <p>This page is <code>templates/Markup.rocstache</code>. It includes the box with
  <code>&#123;&#123;&gt; Hello hello&#125;&#125;</code>: the template <code>Hello</code>, given the
  field <code>hello</code> as its data. Templates call templates; that is how every page here
  shares its sidebar (<code>Top.rocstache</code>) and its footer
  (<code>Bottom.rocstache</code>).</p>
  <p>A markup edit never runs a compiler: roux makes new bytecode in under a millisecond
  and the app swaps it in while it keeps serving. Try breaking the HTML instead, or a tag:
  write <code>&#123;&#123; name</code> without its braces. roux refuses it, names the line, and
  the page keeps the last good version.</p>
</section>
{{> Bottom nav}}
